# Build context for the CrossCheck image.
#
# This is an allow-list, not a deny-list: everything is excluded, then the handful of
# paths the build genuinely reads are added back. A deny-list would quietly rot — every
# new directory (a corpus, a results dump, a notebook) would start shipping to the daemon
# until somebody noticed. The allow-list makes the build's inputs explicit instead, and
# any new build input has to be added here deliberately.
#
# It matters here more than usual. The working tree carries a 1.5 GB `.venv` and a 134 MB
# `.mypy_cache`, and this repo lives on the Windows filesystem via WSL, where the daemon
# reads the context one small file at a time. Allow-listing takes the context from ~1.7 GB
# to under 1 MB.

*

# Dependency resolution: uv installs from the lock, and pyproject's `readme` field means
# the wheel build reads README.md — without it `uv sync` fails on a missing file.
!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
!README.md

# The package itself. `scripts/` is deliberately absent: those are repo-maintenance tools that
# write into benchmarks/, not runtime code, and the model warm-up the container runs is the
# `crosscheck warm-models` CLI command rather than a script.
!src

# The demo page. Unlike the package it is not installed into site-packages — Streamlit runs a
# script by path — so it has to be copied in as source.
!ui

# The reports the demo falls back to when no service is reachable. Only the JSON is needed: the
# corpora and gold sets are not read by explorer mode, so they stay out of the image.
!benchmarks/**/report.json

# Re-excluded inside the allow-listed trees: compiled bytecode is regenerated in the image
# (UV_COMPILE_BYTECODE=1) and would otherwise bust the layer cache on every local test run.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
